        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
The objective of this experiment was to build a detailed quantitative model of cardiac muscle, which included electrophysiological properties, cellular Calcium (Ca)-regulation and contractile activation and relaxation. <P>

The Specific Aims were:<BR>
<Li> To develop a more up-to-date electrophysiological model of cardiac myocyte dynamics;
<LI> To incorporate new Ca-transport data on SR Ca-uptake, release and Sodium (Na)/Ca exchange;
<LI> To extend the model to include cooperative Ca-dependent myofilaments activation, contraction and relaxation
<LI> Implement this model on highly accessible formats (one user-friendly and one computer-friendly for interfacing with other models). </LI><P>

Lack of integration of information from the level of ion channels to Ca transients to myofilament force and shortening was what initiated this investigation.  This experiment developed a comprehensive quantitative model that incorporated up-to-date information on ion channels, modulation of Ca-cycling processes and myofilament activation. The approach involves a team of investigators with long-standing laboratory and modeling experience in each of these processes. Drs. Bers and Puglisi of Loyola University have extensive experience in modeling and quantitative experimental studies of regulation of Ca-cycling and membrane currents in the cardiac myocyte. Similarly, Drs. De Tombe and Solaro of the University of Illinois at Chicago have extensive experimental and modeling experience that focuses on the modulation of myocyte response to Ca. <P>

The approach involved the generation of computer models constrained by dynamic data generated in these and other laboratories. A preliminary working model called LabHEART4 showed feasibility and utility of the proposed modeling format. This endeavor provided new insights into normal physiological regulation of myocyte activity as well as providing a baseline from which altered function induced by alterations in hemodynamic loading was readily considered. A fully integrated model of cardiac myocyte activity and regulation of myocyte activity developed had a broad application to acute changes in the environment as occurs in space travel and re-entry. This experiment had resulting significance to the understanding of the effects of altered gene and protein expression associated with long-term changes in cardiac loading that occur during space travel and return to Earth. 
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
This is a National Space Biomedical Research Institute (NSBRI) ground-based investigation and NASA does not currently have an agreement to archive this data in the LSDA.
        </div>

